1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Client tools and server tools give you the same type of result, but they are different.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,000
And that difference is who runs the code.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
One executes in your application, the other executes on Anthropx infrastructure.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Knowing which is which changes what your loop has to do.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
This lesson maps that line.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Let's anchor three terms before we go deeper.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
First, a client tool.

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
That's any tool that executes inside your own application.

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Whether you defined it yourself or it uses an Anthropx provided schema.

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Second, a server tool.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
That's a tool that runs on Anthropx infrastructure where no handler of yours runs at all.

12
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Third, stop reason tool use.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
That's the signal in Claude's response telling you it wants a client tool executed

14
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
and is pausing for you to run it and hand back a result.

15
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Client tool for where you execute.

16
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Server tool for where Anthropx executes.

17
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
And stop reason tool use for the pause that puts the ball in your court.

18
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Hold those three and the rest of this lesson clicks.

19
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Let's put the two side by side because the whole topic rests on this one distinction.

20
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
With a client side tool, the code runs in your application.

21
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Claude sends back a stop reason of tool use.

22
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
You execute the call yourself and you return a tool result.

23
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
So the conversation can continue.

24
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Nothing happens until your code acts.

25
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
With a server side tool, the code runs on Anthropx infrastructure instead.

26
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
You don't write a handler and you don't send a tool result

27
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
because the result is already computed and returned to you in the response.

28
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
So the question to always ask is simple.

29
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Whose machine runs this tool?

30
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
If it's yours, you're on the hook to execute and reply.

31
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
If it's Anthropx, the work is already done.

32
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Let's trace what actually happens with a client tool step by step.

33
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
It's a round trip between Claude and your code.

34
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Claude decides it needs the tool and returns a response with stop reason tool use,

35
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
carrying the tool's name and the input it wants you to use.

36
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Your application takes that input, runs the real function,

37
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
and captures whatever comes back.

38
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Then you package that output into a tool result block

39
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
and send it to Claude in the next message.

40
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Claude reads the result and either answers the user or reaches for another tool.

41
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The key thing to notice is that your code is doing the executing.

42
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Claude asked, but nothing runs until your application actually runs it and reports back.

43
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Now, four client tools ship with Anthropx-defined schemas

44
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
and they sit firmly on the client side of this map.

45
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Bash runs shell commands.

46
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Text Editor views and edits files.

47
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Memory reads and writes persistent notes across turns.

48
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
And computer use controls a screen, a mouse, and a keyboard.

49
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
You already know the trap here from last lesson.

50
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
A pre-built schema doesn't mean Anthropx runs the tool.

51
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
All four execute in your environment on your side.

52
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
So Claude returns tool use just like any custom tool

53
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
and your code does the work and returns the tool result.

54
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Pass Bash without wiring up a shell executor

55
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
and the conversation just stalls at the tool use pause,

56
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
waiting on a tool result your app never produces.

57
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,000
The schema saves you design work,

58
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
but the runtime responsibility, sandboxing included, stays with you.

59
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
On the other side, you've got the server tools

60
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,000
and these genuinely run on Anthropx infrastructure.

61
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Web search and web fetch pull in live content from the web.

62
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Code execution runs code in a hosted sandbox

63
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
and tool search lets Claude discover relevant tools on demand

64
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
instead of loading them all up front.

65
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
For every one of these, you write no handler at all.

66
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
There's no stop reason tool use pause

67
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
and there's no tool result for you to assemble.

68
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Claude calls the tool, Anthropx runs it,

69
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
and the result comes straight back attached to the response.

70
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,000
That's the whole appeal.

71
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
For capabilities Anthropx already hosts,

72
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,000
you get the behavior without writing

73
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
or running any execution code yourself.

74
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
There's one wrinkle that breaks the clean split,

75
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
so let's be precise about it.

76
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Normally, a server tool runs on Anthropx side with no pause,

77
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:28,000
but if a server tool ends up grouped in parallel with a client tool

78
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
in the same batch of calls, the behavior changes.

79
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,000
The whole group falls back to stop reason tool use.

80
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
That means Claude hands the batch back to you

81
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
and your loop drives execution for every call in it,

82
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
including the server tool that would normally have run on its own.

83
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
So the rule to remember is this,

84
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,000
a mixed batch, one that pairs a client tool with a server tool,

85
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
always pauses for your code.

86
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
When in doubt, assume the client tool behavior wins in a parallel group.

87
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Let's pull out three consequences this split has for how you build.

88
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
First, you write fewer handlers than you'd think.

89
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
You only need execution code for client tools.

90
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Server tools need none because Anthropx runs them.

91
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Second, results arrive by two different paths.

92
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Client results are ones you assemble and send back in a tool result.

93
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Server results show up in line, already attached to the response you got.

94
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Third, tools aren't free even when unused.

95
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
The moment you provide any tools,

96
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:38,000
you pay a standing tool use system prompt cost whatever tool choice you set, even none.

97
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
We'll put a number on that overhead and show how to trim it

98
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
when we get to right-sizing the tool surface.

99
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Fewer handlers, two result paths, and a standing token cost.

100
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Keep those three in view.

101
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
So how do you decide which kind of tool to reach for?

102
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Let it follow from what you need and who should run it.

103
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
When Anthropx already hosts the capability,

104
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
like searching the web or running code,

105
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:07,000
reach for the matching server tool and skip writing any execution code.

106
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
When you need to touch your own systems, your database, your internal API, your files,

107
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
that's a client tool because only your application can reach those.

108
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
And keep one limit front of mind.

109
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Server tools run on Anthropx's side,

110
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
so they can't see your private environment or your internal data.

111
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Match the runtime location to the job.

112
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
Hosted capability, server tool.

113
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Your own systems, client tool. Let's recap.

114
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Client tools and server tools differ on one thing, where the code runs.

115
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Client tools run in your app, so you execute them

116
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:51,000
and return a tool result after a stop reason tool use pause.

117
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Server tools run on Anthropx infrastructure with results returned directly.

118
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Keep in mind, a server tool in a parallel group falls back to tool use.

119
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
That covers client tools versus server tools at runtime.

120
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:07,000
I'll see you in the next one.

